KZ2 patch 7021: exact vector forms of the exponent-255 lanes of fa, fs, fm, fma, fms, fnms and the four
float compares (on by default since an earlier change; KZ2_SPU_XRV=0 turns them off, "t" follows the same-run toggle of
port/kz2/spu/kz2_ab_toggle.c), and
the lanes of each KZ3_SPU_XRSTATS site, and KZ2_SPU_XRV=c, which runs both paths on the game's operands and logs any lane
that differs ([spu-xrv-check], diagnostic).
The SPU float unit has no Inf/NaN (exponent 255 is an ordinary binade, truncation, saturation at +-Smax, exponent 0 is
zero); KZ3 patch 1071 recomputes such lanes one at a time with PR #287's integer lane code, 1089 handles zeros, far
sums and saturated products inline. The rest went out of line lane by lane, and an fma vector with one such lane went
down the whole fallback chain (double path, the TwoSum retry, four libm-or-hardware fmaf, then the lane recompute).
KZ2's VertexJob normalises zero-length vectors (the squared length splatted to all four lanes: rsqrt estimate of 0, an
exponent-255 word, through fi, fm, fnms, fma) and UpdateJob divides by zero denominators (frest of 0, then fcgt on
-Smax), whole vectors at a time: 12 % of VertexJob's host time (spu rounds 32, 33).
Here, with bit 1 of g_kz3_spu_xrq set: the same values from double arithmetic, four lanes at once (each single is exact
as a double, the product is exact, TwoSum gives the exact sum as s + e, truncation is s with its low 29 bits cleared,
one ulp less when they are zero and e has the other sign; saturation, +0 below 2^-126), and the compares as integer
keys inline. Bit for bit the 1071/1089 results: tools/spu/test_spu_xrv.sh (every exponent-255 word against a partner
set, and random words of every class, both fma modes, -msse4.1 and -mfma). With the probes (KZ3_SPU_XRSTATS,
KZ3_SPU_FMASTATS) on, the old paths run, so they count the same lanes. KZ3_SPU_XRQ=0 still turns off 1089's inline lanes.

diff --git a/runtime/spu/spu_helpers.h b/runtime/spu/spu_helpers.h
--- a/runtime/spu/spu_helpers.h
+++ b/runtime/spu/spu_helpers.h
@@ -371,13 +371,130 @@
     *m &= ~(unsigned)_mm_movemask_ps(_mm_castsi128_ps(done));
     return _mm_castsi128_ps(_mm_blendv_epi8(_mm_castps_si128(r), v, done));
